//! Byte-for-byte comparison against captured `ffprobe` output.
//!
//! GENERATED BY OBSERVATION, NOT BY HAND. Every byte in [`STREAMS_DEFAULT`],
//! [`FORMAT_DEFAULT`], [`FORMAT_INI`] and [`FORMAT_XML`] is the exact stdout of
//! one `ffprobe` invocation under `LC_ALL=C`.
//!
//! Reference: `ffprobe version 8.1` (Homebrew, arm64 macOS, 2026),
//! `libavformat 62.12.100`.
//!
//! The sample file, and the three invocations:
//!
//! ```sh
//! ffmpeg -f lavfi -i testsrc2=size=320x240:rate=25:duration=2 \
//!        -f lavfi -i sine=frequency=440:duration=2 \
//!        -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -c:a aac -shortest av.mp4
//!
//! ffprobe -v quiet -of default -show_streams av.mp4
//! ffprobe -v quiet -of default -show_format  av.mp4
//! ffprobe -v quiet -of json    -show_format -show_streams av.mp4
//! ```
//!
//! # Why there is no demuxer here
//!
//! `vaco-demux-mp4` is being written concurrently, so this replays the
//! container's *description* rather than reading the file: [`av_mp4_streams`]
//! builds the `Stream` values a correct MP4 demuxer must produce, taken from
//! the same `ffprobe` run. That deliberately isolates the thing this crate owns
//! — which fields, in what order, spelled how — from the thing it does not.
//! When the demuxer lands, the same expectations run against real bytes and any
//! new difference is the demuxer's.
//!
//! # What this measures
//!
//! [`divergences`] is the honest output of this file: every line where we and
//! the reference differ, with a reason. It is asserted **exactly** — a
//! divergence that disappears fails the test just as loudly as a new one, so
//! the list cannot quietly grow or go stale.

#![allow(
    clippy::expect_used,
    reason = "a test that cannot set up is a failed test"
)]

